WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.200
A chicken virus causes cancer using a gene healthy birds already carry.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.580
How does a cell's own switch get stuck on, and how many copies does it take?

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.480
Let us watch one growth switch inside a single cell to find out.

4
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:20.963
A healthy cell divides only when told to.

5
00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:25.863
Every growth pathway therefore uses an on-step and an off-step.

6
00:00:27.063 --> 00:00:28.863
A growth signal flips the switch on.

7
00:00:30.043 --> 00:00:33.083
When the signal leaves, an off-switch flips it back off.

8
00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:39.063
A normal gene of this kind, able to become a cancer gene when altered, is a

9
00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:40.063
proto-oncogene.

10
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:46.827
Surprisingly, cancer genes are rarely foreign invaders, but altered copies of the

11
00:00:46.827 --> 00:00:51.087
cell's own genes. Take the signalling kinase called Src.

12
00:00:52.347 --> 00:00:54.287
Normally a phosphate sits on its tail.

13
00:00:55.607 --> 00:00:59.767
Another part of the same protein grips that phosphate, holding the kinase shut.

14
00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:03.927
Replace that tail, and the grip has nothing to hold.

15
00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:09.647
The kinase runs all the time, and that altered gene copy is an oncogene.

16
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.770
A second switch jams differently.

17
00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:15.890
Ras is on while it holds GTP.

18
00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:20.130
It turns itself off by breaking that GTP down.

19
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.810
A helper protein called a GAP triggers the breakdown.

20
00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.230
One changed amino acid gets in the helper's way.

21
00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:36.290
Ras then breaks its GTP down only slowly, even with a GAP, so it stays on.

22
00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:38.933
Now count the gene copies.

23
00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:43.973
You carry two copies of nearly every gene, one from each parent.

24
00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:48.793
Alter one copy of Ras, and its protein signals nonstop.

25
00:01:50.013 --> 00:01:53.133
The normal protein from the other copy cannot turn it off.

26
00:01:54.253 --> 00:01:56.173
That is a dominant gain of function.

27
00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.747
There is a second route.

28
00:02:00.027 --> 00:02:03.887
Instead of jamming Ras, remove its off-switch, the GAP helper.

29
00:02:05.227 --> 00:02:09.867
A gene whose loss lets cells grow when they should not is a tumour suppressor.

30
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:13.847
Suppose a cell loses one of its two off-switch copies.

31
00:02:15.087 --> 00:02:16.307
Does the switch stick on?

32
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:27.873
Usually, no, because one working copy still makes enough helper protein to clear the

33
00:02:27.873 --> 00:02:33.913
signal. So the switch stays mostly off; losing one copy stays hidden at the cell

34
00:02:33.913 --> 00:02:39.953
level. Only when the cell loses the second copy does the off-step fail completely.

35
00:02:40.873 --> 00:02:43.073
That is a recessive loss of function.

36
00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:49.823
Jamming an on-step needs only one altered copy, because the normal protein from the

37
00:02:49.823 --> 00:02:52.343
other copy cannot switch the stuck one off.

38
00:02:53.723 --> 00:02:58.783
Removing an off-step usually needs both copies lost, because one good copy still

39
00:02:58.783 --> 00:02:59.783
clears the signal.

40
00:03:01.023 --> 00:03:05.463
One altered copy jams a switch, while off-switch loss usually needs both.
